00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.480
Leah: So, to illustrate the principles
of Single Responsibility Principle

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.719
[SRP], Separation of Concerns [SoC], and
Abstraction, let’s imagine we want to make

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:14.390
a class which calculates pay after tax.

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:15.860
Look at this example.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:25.159
As you can see from this example,
our class is responsible for

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:29.160
doing many things, not all of them
related to the main purpose: ‘to

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.029
calculate the pay after taxes’.

00:00:31.639 --> 00:00:35.830
This could increase the chances of a
bug or make it more complicated to adapt

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.090
when changes or updates are needed.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.800
To simplify this, we can apply Single
Responsibility Principles [SRP]

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.290
gathering things together that change
for the same reasons, and Separation

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:54.680
of Concern principles [SoC] separating
those that change for different reasons.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:00.170
So, let’s see this example
again but using SRP and SoC

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.540
Here, we have collected all the
things that are relevant to the

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.300
single responsibility of the class
– calculating the pay after tax.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.010
This adheres to SRP.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.780
We have also created separate classes
which could change for different

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:23.650
reasons, for example using different
code to generate the payslip or changing

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.699
delivery method from email to post.

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:27.179
This adheres to SoC.

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:33.230
Finally using this example again, we
apply the principle of Abstraction

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.979
which involves hiding things which are
not required, outside of the class.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.949
There may be information which we do
not want to complicate the code or

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:45.690
which may be sensitive in nature which
can be hidden outside of the class.

00:01:46.199 --> 00:01:50.420
In this example, we hide these as
private attributes, so we would

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.530
only have access to the pay after
taxes from outside of the class.

